{"content": {"summary": {"value": "The  summary of the reviewers' concerns that informed my suggested decision for this paper is shown as follows: 1) The novelty lies mainly in the unified taxonomy and integration, rather than fundamentally new techniques. While the taxonomy and cleaning pipeline are well-executed, the core idea of automated benchmark filtering is present in prior works; 2) Circular validation akin to training on the test set and problematic pipeline design and selection-induced inflation of separability."}, "reviewer_concerns": {"value": "The concerns from Reviewer njxr are addressed by the rebuttal, i.e., the authors give the code of the pipeline or dataset. Providing this to determine how easy others can use the pipeline and to assess the quality.

The concerns from  Reviewer ajWx and Reviewer qdgh are still outstanding, i.e., The novelty lies mainly in the unified taxonomy and integration, rather than fundamentally new techniques. While the taxonomy and cleaning pipeline are well-executed, the core idea of automated benchmark filtering is present in prior works. Circular validation akin to training on the test set and problematic pipeline design and selection-induced inflation of separability."}, "reviewer_scores": {"value": "The concerns from Reviewer njxr is addressed by the authors, i.e., the authors give the code of the pipeline or dataset. Providing this to determine how easy others can use the pipeline and to assess the quality. Therefore, I think this reviewer tend to raise the score even this reviewer did not give further response.

The concerns from  Reviewer ajWx and Reviewer qdgh are not addressed by the reviewer, i.e., the novelty lies mainly in the unified taxonomy and integration, rather than fundamentally new techniques. While the taxonomy and cleaning pipeline are well-executed, the core idea of automated benchmark filtering is present in prior works. Circular validation akin to training on the test set and problematic pipeline design and selection-induced inflation of separability. Therefore, I think these reviewer tend to keep their original scores even they did not give further responses."}}, "id": "VVTVe4aeKl", "forum": "owFVvaLFdw", "replyto": "owFVvaLFdw", "signatures": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8036/Area_Chair_vrbB"], "nonreaders": [], "readers": ["everyone"], "writers": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8036/Senior_Area_Chairs", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8036/Area_Chair_vrbB"], "number": 1, "invitations": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8036/-/Meta_Review", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"], "domain": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "tcdate": 1766562862422, "cdate": 1766562862422, "tmdate": 1770619145425, "mdate": 1770619145425, "parentInvitations": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Meta_Review", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "version": 2, "_normalized_invitation": "iclr.cc/2026/conference/submission8036/-/meta_review"}

{"content": {"summary": {"value": "This paper presents Matting Anything 2, a versatile video matting model leveraging SAM2 for flexible user prompts. 

Initial reviews highlighted significant concerns regarding novelty relative to SAM2, evaluation fairness, dataset detail, and validation on real-world videos. The authors provided a comprehensive rebuttal, clarifying the architectural novelty of the trimap-based pipeline, adding a detailed dataset breakdown, providing qualitative real-world results, and conducting a comparative experiment with aligned training data. These responses resolved most concerns, e.g., those raised by Reviewers Uqg2 and TLqf.

Key strengths are the effective technical contributions and strong experimental results. A primary unresolved weakness, noted by Reviewer Qe9K, is the lack of quantitative evaluation on non-synthetic real-world videos, which limits full validation of the "matting anything" claim. This gap in real-world performance evidence remains a notable limitation."}, "reviewer_concerns": {"value": "See above."}, "reviewer_scores": {"value": "While some reviewers may revise their scores positively, others may not, as there is a lack of quantitative evaluation on real-world, non-synthetic videos."}}, "id": "NyNn4OWFFI", "forum": "6K08FPo2cf", "replyto": "6K08FPo2cf", "signatures": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission293/Area_Chair_aJQp"], "nonreaders": [], "readers": ["everyone"], "writers": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission293/Senior_Area_Chairs", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission293/Area_Chair_aJQp"], "number": 1, "invitations": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission293/-/Meta_Review", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"], "domain": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "tcdate": 1767618122800, "cdate": 1767618122800, "tmdate": 1770618778954, "mdate": 1770618778954, "parentInvitations": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Meta_Review", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "version": 2, "_normalized_invitation": "iclr.cc/2026/conference/submission293/-/meta_review"}

{"content": {"summary": {"value": "This paper presents an attack framework for watermark removal and forgery that operates in a no-box setting using only a single watermarked image. The problem is timely and practically relevant, and reviewers appreciated the unified approach to both removal and forgery attacks. 

That said, reviewers raised several concerns that limited their enthusiasm about the paper. The primary issue is that the method's scope is limited to post-processing watermarks, while the framing suggests broader applicability. The author's exclusive focus on post-processing watermarks needs a better motivation. Furthermore, the authors acknowledge their approach does not perform well on in-processing methods like Tree-Ring, which limits the generality of the contribution given the growing importance of such methods. Reviewers also felt the evaluation could be more comprehensive, suggesting additional baselines and attack methods. The original submission computed improvements against weak baselines, and when measured against the second-best method, the gains are more modest. Some experimental details were also missing or unclear, though the authors were responsive in clarifying these during the rebuttal. Notably, there was no strong champion for this paper."}, "reviewer_concerns": {"value": "The authors addressed concerns about missing experimental parameters and committed to revising the overreaching claims. They also conducted additional experiments on requested baselines during the rebuttal, which was appreciated.

The main outstanding issue is the scope limitation. While post-processing watermarks remain practically important, the limited effectiveness on in-processing methods constrains the overall contribution. The supplementary experiments were helpful but would benefit from integration into the main part of a revised paper. When checking the revised paper, I could not find the additional watermarking baselines (VINE, Gaussian Shading, Stable Signature) that were shown in the rebuttal and the additional attack methods (rotation, brightness+contrast, cropping) are also not integrated into the paper."}, "reviewer_scores": {"value": "Reviewer QP2W (score 6) engaged constructively and would likely maintain their score since they were already borderline in their initial review. The reviewer acknowledged the authors' responsiveness but noted that core issues remain. Reviewers DC2f (score 4) and PmLJ (score 2) raised many concerns most of which were addressed by the authors, but neither reviewer participated in the discussion. As noted by the authors, PmLJ's review contained factual errors but even after excluding it, the remaining reviews do not champion acceptance."}}, "id": "Gjwxgdpkbq", "forum": "dOxOX4GF1k", "replyto": "dOxOX4GF1k", "signatures": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12929/Area_Chair_meFk"], "nonreaders": [], "readers": ["everyone"], "writers": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12929/Senior_Area_Chairs", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12929/Area_Chair_meFk"], "number": 1, "invitations": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12929/-/Meta_Review", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"], "domain": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "tcdate": 1767812343698, "cdate": 1767812343698, "tmdate": 1770619367559, "mdate": 1770619367559, "parentInvitations": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Meta_Review", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "version": 2, "_normalized_invitation": "iclr.cc/2026/conference/submission12929/-/meta_review"}

{"content": {"summary": {"value": "Reviewers had a number of major concerns about the paper ranging from a lack of adequete comparisons to visual representation learning in reinforcement learning, a lack of evaluation of OOD generalization of the approach, a lack of adequete discussion of related work, and the scarcity of the required triple data needed to train the approach. The authors were able to address some of the concerns, such as adding an additional baseline to the visual representation in reinforcement learning. However, the remaining set of concerns in the paper makes it so that the paper is not ready for publication in the current state."}, "reviewer_concerns": {"value": "Reviewers had a number of major concerns about the paper ranging from a lack of adequete comparisons to visual representation learning in reinforcement learning, a lack of evaluation of OOD generalization of the approach, a lack of adequate discussion of related work, and the scarcity of the required triple data needed to train the approach, as well as various textual clarifications. 

Reviewers were able to partially address some of the concerns on textual clarifications as well as adding a CURL baseline. However, a more extensive comparison to other visual representation learning methods in RL is missing, as well as a revised discussion about these baselines in the related work. Finally, concerns about the evaluation of the OOD generalization of approach is not addressed."}, "reviewer_scores": {"value": "I believe that the concerns of Reviewer iVWx was not adequetly addressed, with no additional evaluations of OOD generalization, and believe the score would remain a reject. In addition, the major concern of Reviewer 6vmd on comparisons with other relevant baselines is also not adequetly addressed, with only one additional set of results using the CURL method. Finally, a major concern of Reviewer  eqoC
 on the scarcity of triple data is not well addressed, with benchmarks such as RL-Bench and Mani-skill not having dense reward data that could be used to learn this foveal attention layer."}}, "id": "VUS7ddx0Uu", "forum": "KJ0ktHAuds", "replyto": "KJ0ktHAuds", "signatures": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23632/Area_Chair_Ahwv"], "nonreaders": [], "readers": ["everyone"], "writers": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23632/Senior_Area_Chairs", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23632/Area_Chair_Ahwv"], "number": 1, "invitations": ["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23632/-/Meta_Review", "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"], "domain": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference", "tcdate": 1767750985063, "cdate": 1767750985063, "tmdate": 1770619802511, "mdate": 1770619802511, "parentInvitations": "ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Meta_Review", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "version": 2, "_normalized_invitation": "iclr.cc/2026/conference/submission23632/-/meta_review"}
